<section id="cx-evidence" class="cx-section-forest">
  <div class="cx-wrap">
    <h2 class="cx-title">What is really flowing into Sydney's drinking water</h2>

    <div class="cx-bignum-band">
      <div class="cx-bignum">
        <div class="cx-bignum-pre">More than</div>
        <div class="cx-bignum-val"><span data-count="20">20</span><span class="cx-bignum-unit">billion litres</span></div>
        <div class="cx-bignum-label">of polluted mine water per year are pumped out of mines in the Gardens of Stone area. A lot of this water ends up in the Coxs River catchment and is only semi-treated, still carrying salts and heavy metals.<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></sup><sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-5" aria-label="Source 5">[5]</a></sup></div>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-bignum">
        <div class="cx-bignum-pre">An estimated</div>
        <div class="cx-bignum-val"><span data-count="80">80</span>&ndash;<span data-count="90">90</span>%</div>
        <div class="cx-bignum-label">of the dry-weather flow in the upper to mid Coxs River catchment is mine water, not river water<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-2" aria-label="Source 2">[2]</a></sup></div>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-bignum cx-bignum-alert">
        <div class="cx-bignum-pre">In 2023&ndash;24 alone</div>
        <div class="cx-bignum-val"><span data-count="5.7" data-decimals="1">5.7</span><span class="cx-bignum-unit">billion litres</span></div>
        <div class="cx-bignum-label">of polluted mine water discharged into local creeks.<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-3" aria-label="Source 3">[3]</a></sup></div>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-bignum">
        <div class="cx-bignum-pre">Three mines plan to run until</div>
        <div class="cx-bignum-val">2040<span class="cx-bignum-unit">&amp; 2042</span></div>
        <div class="cx-bignum-label">one to 2040, two to 2042, and each needs only a modification to get there<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-4" aria-label="Source 4">[4]</a></sup></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="cx-flow-block">
      <h3>From clean catchment to polluted river in four steps</h3>
      <p class="cx-flow-intro">Rain that falls on the Gardens of Stone soaks through sandstone into groundwater, which is why water from natural catchments like this one is normally pristine. Here's what changes:</p>
      <ol class="cx-flow-steps">
        <li class="cx-flow-step">
          <span class="cx-flow-badge">1</span>
          <h4>Pumped out</h4>
          <p>Groundwater floods the mine workings and is pumped up from deep underground and off the Newnes Plateau, more than 20 billion litres a year.<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-1" aria-label="Source 1">[1]</a></sup></p>
        </li>
        <li class="cx-flow-step">
          <span class="cx-flow-badge">2</span>
          <h4>Semi-treated</h4>
          <p>Reverse osmosis is a process for cleaning water that strips out most of the salt and dissolved metals, but around a third of the water is only filtered, so the salt and heavy metals stay in. The two are mixed, and about 18.5 million litres a day of that semi-treated mix is released, still carrying salinity and metals.<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-5" aria-label="Source 5">[5]</a></sup></p>
        </li>
        <li class="cx-flow-step">
          <span class="cx-flow-badge">3</span>
          <h4>Brine left behind</h4>
          <p>Evaporation leaves a toxic brine. Groundwater contaminated by it leaches into Wangcol Creek, then the Coxs River.<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-5" aria-label="Source 5">[5]</a></sup></p>
        </li>
        <li class="cx-flow-step cx-flow-step-alert">
          <span class="cx-flow-badge">4</span>
          <h4>Into the river</h4>
          <p>In 2023&ndash;24, 5.7 billion litres of polluted mine water was discharged into local creeks.<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-3" aria-label="Source 3">[3]</a></sup></p>
        </li>
      </ol>
      <div class="cx-ps-panel">
        <h4 class="cx-ps-title">The power station</h4>
        <p>The brine is mixed with fly ash and placed in ash repositories at Mt Piper power station. The older heap was built without a base liner, according to the operator's own consultants, and leachate from it is still being detected in groundwater.<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-13" aria-label="Source 13">[13]</a></sup> Since 2022, brine-mixed ash has gone into a newer, lined repository, and the old heap has now largely been capped.<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-13" aria-label="Source 13">[13]</a></sup> The operator also says the liners of two of its brine waste ponds may be compromised, with replacement scheduled for 2026/27.<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-13" aria-label="Source 13">[13]</a></sup> Community groups say toxic brine is being squeezed into the aquifer that feeds Wangcol Creek, and report years of complaints about illegal, unlicensed and excessive discharges from the power station.<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-11" aria-label="Source 11">[11]</a></sup></p>
        <div class="cx-ps-stats">
          <div class="cx-ps-stat"><div class="cx-ps-num">9&times;</div><div class="cx-ps-lab">the 350 &micro;S/cm guideline for upland streams: Wangcol Creek salinity reached 3,298 &micro;S/cm on 10 December 2025<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-11" aria-label="Source 11">[11]</a></sup></div></div>
          <div class="cx-ps-stat"><div class="cx-ps-num">1,000+</div><div class="cx-ps-lab">&micro;S/cm: salinity at the downstream Wangcol Creek gauge often exceeded this in 2023 to 2025, in the operator's own monitoring<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-13" aria-label="Source 13">[13]</a></sup></div></div>
          <div class="cx-ps-stat"><div class="cx-ps-num">30 yrs</div><div class="cx-ps-lab">Dr Ian Wright has studied the catchment. He calls the Wangcol Creek discharge point &ldquo;the worst water pollution site in Sydney's drinking water catchment&rdquo; he has seen<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-12" aria-label="Source 12">[12]</a></sup></div></div>
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>

    <details class="cx-evid-detail">
      <summary>Read the full detail</summary>
      <p class="cx-lede">Rain that falls on the Gardens of Stone soaks through sandstone into groundwater, which is why water from natural catchments like this one is normally clean enough to drink. Intensive underground mining breaks that cycle. Groundwater floods into the mine workings and is pumped out from deep underground, millions of litres every day. By the time it reaches the surface it has been through coal seams and mine workings, and it carries salt and heavy metals such as arsenic and selenium.</p>
      <p class="cx-lede">During low flow periods, mine water discharge makes up an estimated 80 to 90 percent of the Coxs River's baseflow in the upper to mid-catchment, according to Lithgow Environment Group's own water monitoring. Much of what's actually flowing here isn't natural river water at all, it's mine water, flowing on toward Warragamba Dam. Local environmental groups have been monitoring this catchment for over twenty years, blowing the whistle on repeated discharges that have cost mining companies almost $1.9 million in fines and penalties.</p>
      <p class="cx-lede">The mine water is sent to a treatment plant that is approved to take up to 42 million litres of mine water a day.<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-16" aria-label="Source 16">[16]</a></sup> About 28.8 million litres a day goes through reverse osmosis. The other 13.2 million litres a day, nearly a third, is only filtered. The two are mixed, and about 18.5 million litres a day of that semi-treated water is released into the catchment. It still carries salt and heavy metals, and the Gardens of Stone Alliance says this release has neither planning consent nor an EPA licence. The concentrated brine left behind goes to the power station, and toxic groundwater contaminated by the brine leaches into Wangcol Creek and then the Coxs River.<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-5" aria-label="Source 5">[5]</a></sup></p>
      <p class="cx-lede">And the mines want to keep going. Three mines in this catchment plan to apply to extend their lives, one to 2040 and two to 2042.</p>
    </details>

    <div class="cx-eco-block">
      <h3>The Platypus,<br>Biladurang in Wiradjuri, are gone.<br>So is what they eat.<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-10" aria-label="Source 10">[10]</a></sup></h3>
      <p>Independent university research has confirmed platypus have vanished entirely from stretches of the Coxs River near Lithgow, driven out by salinity and heavy metal pollution.<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-6" aria-label="Source 6">[6]</a></sup> The reason is simple: without healthy populations of yabbies, mayflies and dragonflies, there's nothing left for a platypus to eat. Peer-reviewed studies of this catchment have found the same pollution significantly reduces the abundance and diversity of the small invertebrate life that the entire river food chain depends on.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>


<section id="cx-loophole" class="cx-section-loophole">
  <div class="cx-wrap">
    <div class="cx-kicker">The loophole</div>
    <h2 class="cx-title">The law meant to stop this has a hole in it</h2>
    <p class="cx-standout-lede">In 2017, environmental groups won a court case that forced coal mine polluters to clean up their discharge into this catchment. The government's answer was to <span class="cx-lh-red">weaken the law</span>.<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-7" aria-label="Source 7">[7]</a></sup></p>
    <p class="cx-lede">The water quality test that protects Sydney's drinking water, NorBE, was watered down after that court case. When an expansion is put forward, the mining company only has to show its impact on our drinking water will be no worse than the pollution it was already allowed to cause.<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-14" aria-label="Source 14">[14]</a></sup> And that is just one part of the problem. Under our planning laws, if a mining company is modifying an existing mine rather than proposing a new one, it <strong class="cx-lh-red">doesn't have to meet the water quality test at all.</strong> Almost every major mining project in this catchment has been modified a number of times,<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-17" aria-label="Source 17">[17]</a></sup> so the one legal safeguard meant to stop this is being <span class="cx-lh-red">sidestepped by design</span>.</p>

    <div class="cx-stat-row cx-loophole-stats">
      <div class="cx-stat-card">
        <div class="cx-stat-num">~$1.9M</div>
        <div class="cx-stat-label">Fines and penalties for mining companies polluting this catchment since 2015<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-8" aria-label="Source 8">[8]</a></sup></div>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-stat-card">
        <div class="cx-stat-num">~$1B</div>
        <div class="cx-stat-label">Roughly what the company behind these mines earns in a year, by industry estimates<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-9" aria-label="Source 9">[9]</a></sup></div>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-stat-card cx-stat-card-red">
        <div class="cx-stat-num">2040 &amp; 2042</div>
        <div class="cx-stat-label">The years three mines in this catchment plan to apply to keep operating until<sup class="cx-src"><a href="#cx-src-4" aria-label="Source 4">[4]</a></sup></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p class="cx-loophole-close">Even the largest single fine amounted to about 0.1% of a year's revenue. That isn't a deterrent, it's a line item. And while local groups have spent two decades trying to hold this catchment together, the company responsible is asking the government for extensions for three of its mines. <span class="cx-lh-red">Let's keep the bastards honest.</span> Write a submission, make a difference.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="cx-asks" class="cx-section-asks">
  <div class="cx-wrap">
    <div class="cx-kicker">What you can do</div>
    <h2 class="cx-title">Three people can change this.</h2>
    <p class="cx-standout-lede">The Planning Minister and the Water Minister hold the power to strengthen the law and protect the catchment, and your local MP can raise it in Parliament. We have put together examples of statements you can raise, or use as inspiration to write your own. If you receive a response, forward it to <a href="mailto:campaigner@gardensofstone.org.au">campaigner@gardensofstone.org.au</a>. The Alliance can use responses to map political support, inform parliamentary advocacy and demonstrate the strength of community concern. Please remove any personal details you don't want shared.</p>

    <div class="cx-asks-grid">
      <div class="cx-ask-card cx-premier">
        <div class="cx-ask-role">Planning ask</div>
        <h3>Minister for Planning and Public Spaces, Paul Scully</h3>
        <p>The NorBE test sits in planning law. Strengthen it, as your party promised in 2017.</p>
        <p class="cx-sample-text" data-sample="1">"Minister, mine water now makes up an estimated 80 to 90 per cent of dry-weather flow in the upper to mid Coxs River catchment, on its way to Warragamba Dam and the taps of 5,000,000 Sydneysiders. The law meant to protect that water, the Neutral or Beneficial Effect test, was weakened in 2017, and at the time your party committed in Parliament to restore the proper protections if the rules deteriorated. They have deteriorated. Three mines in this catchment plan to apply to extend their lives to 2040 and 2042, and the weakened test, which lets expansions be judged against the pollution already permitted, along with the modification pathway that avoids the test altogether, would allow them to keep discharging polluted mine water for that long. As the Minister responsible for planning law, I'm asking you to keep that promise and strengthen the NorBE test, so it is measured against a clean baseline, requires real treatment of polluted discharge, and applies to expansions of existing mines too."</p>
        <div class="cx-ask-actions">
          <a class="cx-ask-btn" href="https://www.nsw.gov.au/nsw-government/ministers/minister-for-planning-and-public-spaces" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Contact the Minister</a>
          <button class="cx-copy-btn" data-copy-target="1" type="button">Copy text</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-ask-card cx-political">
        <div class="cx-ask-role">Water ask</div>
        <h3>Minister for Water, Rose Jackson</h3>
        <p>Argue for a strong NorBE test, and for independent monitoring of the water Sydney drinks.</p>
        <p class="cx-sample-text" data-sample="2">"Minister, the mines in the Coxs River catchment pump more than 20 billion litres of polluted mine water a year, about 18.5 million litres a day of it is released only semi-treated, still carrying salt and heavy metals, and mine water now makes up an estimated 80 to 90 per cent of the river's dry-weather flow in the upper to mid catchment on its way to Warragamba Dam. Platypus have disappeared from stretches of the river, and mining companies have been fined and penalised almost $1.9 million for discharges into the catchment since 2015. The NorBE test is meant to protect Sydney's drinking water, but it was weakened in 2017. As the Minister for Water, I'm asking you to argue within Government for a strong NorBE test, measured against a clean baseline, with best-practice treatment required wherever polluted water is discharged, and to back independent, public monitoring of water quality in the catchment."</p>
        <div class="cx-ask-actions">
          <a class="cx-ask-btn" href="mailto:office@jackson.minister.nsw.gov.au?subject=Protect%20the%20Coxs%20River%20catchment">Email the Minister</a>
          <button class="cx-copy-btn" data-copy-target="2" type="button">Copy text</button>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="cx-ask-card cx-local">
        <div class="cx-ask-role">Local ask</div>
        <h3>Your local MP</h3>
        <p>Whichever electorate you live in, Sydney's drinking water is your MP's business. First <a href="https://elections.nsw.gov.au/elections/find-my-electorate" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--water);font-weight:600;">find your electorate</a>, then get your MP's email from the <a href="https://www.parliament.nsw.gov.au/members-and-electorates/electorates-elections/electorate-office-contacts" target="_blank" rel="noopener" style="color:var(--water);font-weight:600;">electorate office list</a>.</p>
        <p class="cx-sample-text" data-sample="3">"As a resident of [your electorate], I'm asking you to protect our drinking water by fixing NorBE, and to raise the state of the Coxs River catchment in Parliament. Mine water now dominates stretches of the Coxs River in dry weather, and three mines in the catchment plan to apply to keep operating until 2040 and 2042. The river runs on to Warragamba Dam, which supplies the drinking water of around 5,000,000 people, including your constituents. The test meant to protect that water, the NorBE test, was weakened in 2017. I'd like you to back a stronger test, and to tell me where you stand."</p>
        <div class="cx-ask-actions">
          <a class="cx-ask-btn" href="https://elections.nsw.gov.au/elections/find-my-electorate" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Find your electorate</a>
          <button class="cx-copy-btn" data-copy-target="3" type="button">Copy text</button>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  <p class="cx-asks-contact">Have questions about what to say or how to make your email personal? Contact our campaigner at <a href="mailto:campaigner@gardensofstone.org.au">campaigner@gardensofstone.org.au</a>.</p>
  </div>
</section>

